High-Value DSQ 1: Which bases are problematic?
•	High-Value DSQ: Can we identify harsh base locations that cause parts to wear out faster? By linking deployment history to maintenance records and part lifespan, can we predict which engines are most at risk and adjust our maintenance schedules and supply chain accordingly?
o	Why high-value: It connects Operations (base locations) to Engineering (coating loss) and cost (maintenance hours). One can increase inspections for engines at harsh bases, pre-position spare parts where they are most needed. This could be a system-level question.
o	Data need to join:
1.	ESAS Data: For coating loss measurements.
2.	Aircraft_Installation_History: To know when an engine was at a specific base.
3.	HAMR_ENG_ITEM_LIFE_USE: To get the Cycles and Hours accumulated during that time.
4.	Table with JCN to quantify the number of maintenance actions.
5.	BaseNames: To get the full name of the base.
High-Value DSQ 2: Can we re-use a part?
•	High-Value DSQ: Instead of relying only on flight hours or cycles, can we create a 'Health Score' for every major component? By combining data from inspections, sensors, and past failures, can we predict a failure before it happens and replace the part at the most optimal time? 
o	Why high-value: It moves from reactive (what happened?) to predictive (what will happen?). directly addresses risk and readiness. This is the core of a "predictive maintenance" strategy. It shifts us from being reactive (fixing things after they break) to being proactive, which increases safety, improves fleet readiness, and cuts down on costly unscheduled downtime.
o	Data need to join:
1.	Inspection Data (ESAS, PW Inspect sources). 
2.	Failure Data (FaultCodes, JCN_AEFR_PROB_FAULTS).
3.	Usage Data (HAMR_ENG_ITEM_LIFE_USE).
4.	Installation History (Aircraft_Installation_History).
High-Value DSQ 3: What is the life cycle cost for this part?
•	High-Value DSQ: What is the true lifetime cost of a component, not just its purchase price? If we choose a part with a more advanced (and expensive) coating, does it pay for itself through fewer repairs, longer life, and less inspection time over x years?
o	Why high-value: It connects Engineering/Manufacturing (bill of materials, coating choice) to Finance (cost/hours) and Logistics/Maintenance (repairs, lifespan). This could be a system-level question. 
o	Data need to join:
1.	Bill of Materials / As-Built Data (part_numbers table with Coating_Material).
2.	A part cost table. (We need to find this)
3.	All historical maintenance and inspection tables (JCN, ESAS, etc.) to calculate lifecycle repair costs.
4.	Usage tables (HAMR) to determine lifespan.


